{
  "dom_id": "step-q120275",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14258034/assets/cbd0ffe0fb9e879f2a93557da1aef08622c9e048e14d83c4f4619cf5a1cd47bb.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14258034/assets/fba4b74fde44c862267cd2c58852f4f829f5b07ead948b0c18d79e94f7d1c0fc.png)\n\nIn $△OUW$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OU=2|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OU}$ and $u$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=2u.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $UW=3|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{UW}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{UW}=-3v.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =2u-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v=2u-3v.$\n\nSince $u∦v,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
